# Functional test: show vpn ipsec dataplane sa reads a REAL kernel SAD back.
# Spec: spec-ipsec-dataplane-inspection (AC-1, user story 1)
#
# VALIDATES: after a Child SA establishes on a real kernel, `show vpn ipsec
#            dataplane sa` names the two ESP addresses and the ESP transform
#            THIS test configured, and after `clear vpn ipsec sa` the same
#            command no longer names them.
# PREVENTS: the vacuity a read-only dump invites. A dump command passes on an
#           empty kernel with its whole body deleted, so this file asserts a
#           TRANSITION (present, then absent) over objects the test installed,
#           never a state (ai/rules/interop-and-goal-validation.md).
#
# ipsec-dataplane-show.ci is the sibling that proves REACHABILITY under the noop
# backend, where every dump refuses. This file is the kernel half: it is the only
# place a .ci sees `netlink.XfrmStateList` answer with a record.
#
# WHY ONE DATAPLANE AND NOT TWO. The kernel keys an XFRM state on
# (src, dst, spi, proto), and the two ends of one negotiated Child SA are the
# SAME two states, so a second XfrmStateAdd answers EEXIST. The RESPONDER runs
# the real xfrm backend and the initiator the noop one, exactly as
# ipsec-teardown-leaves-nothing.ci does, so one daemon owns what is in the kernel
# and every assertion below is about that daemon.
#
# WHY THE EXECUTOR IS ON THE RESPONDER. The dump has to read the SAD of the
# daemon that installed it, and the clear has to BE the teardown the kernel sees.
#
# WHY THE SELECTORS ARE RFC 5737 DOCUMENTATION PREFIXES. The installed policies
# are real and node-wide for the life of the test, so the selectors name
# addresses no other test can be using.
#
# Gated option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin: the responder programs real XFRM
# state, so this SKIPs on a host without CAP_NET_ADMIN and runs as root in the
# QEMU VM, where `fsuite ipsec` in internal/le/qemu/alltests.go executes the
# suite.

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
# Serialize against every other real-XFRM ipsec test. `ip xfrm state` is
# NODE-WIDE, so a sibling running concurrently puts its own SPIs in the dump this
# test reads and the absence assertion below would be about the other test's
# kernel state.
option=exclusive:group=ipsec-xfrm
option=env:var=ze_test_ike_port:value=$PORT2
option=timeout:value=120s
option=env:var=ze_log_ike:value=info

# ── Responder daemon (background, REAL xfrm, engine-steps executor): 127.0.0.2 ──
stdin=responder:terminator=EOF_RESPONDER
plugin {
	external engine-steps {
		run "ze-test engine-steps ./engine-steps.json"
		encoder json
	}
}

vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer initiator {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type respond
				local-address 127.0.0.2
				remote-address 127.0.0.1
				mode tunnel
				traffic-selector 1 {
					local {
						prefix 203.0.113.0/24
					}
					remote {
						prefix 198.51.100.0/24
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_RESPONDER

# ── Initiator daemon (background, noop dataplane): binds 127.0.0.1 ──
stdin=initiator:terminator=EOF_INITIATOR
vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer peer-1 {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type initiate
				local-address 127.0.0.1
				remote-address 127.0.0.2
				mode tunnel
				traffic-selector 1 {
					local {
						prefix 198.51.100.0/24
					}
					remote {
						prefix 203.0.113.0/24
					}
				}
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_INITIATOR

# Keep the noop override on the initiator only. env execs ze directly, so
# teardown signals the daemon rather than an intermediate shell.
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=env ze_test_ike_dataplane=noop ze -:stdin=initiator
# The responder runs in the FOREGROUND because it carries the executor: the
# engine steps end with `request shutdown`, so this process exits when they are
# done. Automatic teardown then stops the initiator, with no fixed sleep.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=responder:timeout=110s

# ── Engine steps, dispatched inside the responder ──

# Establishment gate first. aes-cbc appears in the SA row only once IKE_AUTH has
# completed, so this is a real gate on the tunnel and not a delay.
command=show vpn ipsec sa | json
expect=output:contains=aes-cbc:timeout=60

# AC-1: the kernel SAD carries the two ESP states this tunnel installed, and the
# dump names the transform. `cbc(aes)` and `hmac(sha256)` are the kernel's own
# spellings of the aes256 / sha256 proposal above (xfrmEncName, xfrmAuthName in
# internal/component/ike/dataplane/xfrm_linux.go). They come from the netlink
# record, so neither string can be produced by the engine's belief.
command=show vpn ipsec dataplane sa
expect=output:contains=cbc(aes):timeout=45
expect=output:contains=hmac(sha256):timeout=15
# The addresses this test configured, so the assertion names an object the test
# installed rather than whatever the node happens to hold.
expect=output:contains=127.0.0.2:timeout=15
expect=output:contains=127.0.0.1:timeout=15
# The transform came from a kernel record, so the record carries the kernel's own
# fields too. Mode is what Ze asked for and what the kernel stored back.
expect=output:json=sas.0.mode=tunnel:timeout=15

# The TRANSITION. The clear ends the session, and cleanupChild removes the two
# states this daemon installed (established.go -> child.go removeChildSA ->
# dataplane RemoveSA). The dump must then stop naming the transform.
#
# The responder is connection-type respond, so it does not re-initiate on its own,
# and the initiator's reconnect is what eventually brings the tunnel back. The
# poll re-dispatches every 200ms, so the absence is observed inside that window.
# Without the removal this step is the one that reds: the SAD still holds both
# states and cbc(aes) is still in the dump.
command=clear vpn ipsec sa
expect=output:contains=clear-all:timeout=15
command=show vpn ipsec dataplane sa
expect=output:absent=cbc(aes):timeout=45

expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=engine-steps: all steps passed
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
